<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ovambo people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ovambo, Aawambo, Owambo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ovambo, or Aawambo, are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-peoples">Bantu-speaking</a> people of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, the largest ethnic group of Namibia. They lived in a number of kingdoms, such as Ondonga and Kwanyama, on the floodplains around the Kunene and the Cuvelai, farming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a> and keeping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>. Many Ovambo migrant workers supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swapo">SWAPO</a> in the struggle for Namibian independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ovambo-people.jpg" alt="Ovambo people" width="320" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Walther Dobbertin · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_105-DSWA0038,_Deutsch-S%C3%BCd-Westafrika,_Ovambo-M%C3%A4nner.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Ovambo are a group of kindred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-peoples">Bantu-speaking</a> peoples who inhabit what was formerly called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovamboland">Ovamboland</a>, the flat, sandy and grassy plains of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a> and the Cunene Province of southwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>. In Namibia the groups include the Ndonga, Kwanyama, Kwambi, Ngandjera, Mbalantu, Kolonkadhi, Kwaluudhi and Mbandja, and the Kwanyama are the largest. Seasonal rains flood the area through water courses called oshanas, and the Ovambo adapted their housing, agriculture and livestock practices to the changes between wet and dry seasons. They started migrating to the area from the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> around the 14th century and expanded further south into Namibia in the 17th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before the 19th century the Ovambo were largely unaffected by Swahili-Arab and European traders. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> established a colony in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a> in 1884 it left them undisturbed and concentrated on the south and the coast. After the First World War South Africa took over the territory as South West Africa and brought plantations, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> ranching and mining to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovamboland">Ovamboland</a>. In the 1920s and 1930s the Ovambo rose several times against South African rule, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-defence-force-south-africa">Union Defence Force</a> suppressed each rising. A veterinary line, the Police Zone, kept the Ovambo from moving south without permits, and the German and later colonial authorities preserved their traditional authorities to work with.</p><p>Because of a labour shortage, in part a result of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herero-and-nama-genocide">Herero and Nama genocide</a> of 1907–1908, many Ovambo went south as migrant labourers, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/racial-segregation">racial segregation</a> and highly restricted rights. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> introduced apartheid rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovamboland">Ovamboland</a> in 1948. It declared Ovamboland an independent province in 1973 and appointed chiefs aligned with its policies, which the Ovambo rejected, and the appointed chief minister was assassinated in 1975. Together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swapo">SWAPO</a> movement, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a> and its Ovambo people gained independence in 1990. In 1915 Portugal launched a violent campaign in the Ovambo territories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, in which its forces massacred people and burned villages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Ovambo live a settled life based on farming and herding. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">Millet</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorghum">sorghum</a> and beans are the staple crops, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, goats and sheep are kept mainly for milk, with hunting, fishing and gathering as supplements. A homestead is a complex of huts, each with its own purpose, inside a fence of large vertical poles. Each tribe had a hereditary chief, and descent is matrilineal, so that chieftainship passes through the maternal line.</p><p>Traditional belief names a supreme being, Kalunga, with rites centred on a sacred fire. Finnish Lutheran missionaries arrived in the 1870s, and most Ovambo are now Lutheran, with traditional practices such as the sacred fire and invoking Kalunga continuing in a mixed form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ovambo people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Nkhumbi (Humbe) kingdom formed on the middle Kunene river by the 18th century, among cattle-keeping peoples related to the <b>Ovambo</b> and Nyaneka.</blockquote><a href="/state/humbe-kingdom">Humbe Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Ondonga was one of the <b>Ovambo</b> kingdoms on the floodplains south of the Kunene river, formed by the 17th century under rulers drawn from a royal matriclan.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-ondonga">Kingdom of Ondonga</a></li><li><blockquote>It grew into the most populous of the <b>Ovambo</b> kingdoms, with its royal seat in the area of modern Ondjiva.</blockquote><a href="/state/kwanyama-kingdom">Kwanyama Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Land was handed to white farmers, while Africans were confined to reserves and northern <b>Ovambo</b> men supplied contract labour to mines and farms.</blockquote><a href="/state/south-west-africa">South West Africa</a></li></ul></section>
